1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,410
This is one of the most
crowded cities on Earth.

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:07,860
Seven and a half million people, packed
into towers so tall and so close together

3
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:09,870
that some streets never see the sun.

4
00:00:10,771 --> 00:00:14,001
But fly a few minutes out of the center,
and you'll see something strange.

5
00:00:14,411 --> 00:00:15,001
Mountains.

6
00:00:15,331 --> 00:00:15,661
Jungle.

7
00:00:15,831 --> 00:00:16,581
Empty beaches.

8
00:00:16,791 --> 00:00:19,041
Most of Hong Kong isn't city at all.

9
00:00:19,842 --> 00:00:24,072
So how do seven and a half million people
fit into a place that's mostly mountains?

10
00:00:24,382 --> 00:00:28,382
And why does a city with this much empty
land have some of the smallest homes in the

11
00:00:28,422 --> 00:00:28,792
world?

12
00:00:32,785 --> 00:00:33,755
Start with the map.

13
00:00:33,825 --> 00:00:37,015
Hong Kong is about eleven hundred
square kilometers of land:

14
00:00:37,165 --> 00:00:39,555
a peninsula and more
than two hundred islands.

15
00:00:39,765 --> 00:00:43,235
And most of it is steep, rocky hills
that drop straight into the sea.

16
00:00:44,149 --> 00:00:46,639
Only about a quarter of
that land is built on.

17
00:00:46,849 --> 00:00:50,119
The rest is mountains, forests,
reservoirs and wetland.

18
00:00:50,775 --> 00:00:54,235
And about forty percent of Hong
Kong is protected country parks,

19
00:00:54,275 --> 00:00:56,125
where you're not allowed to build at all.

20
00:00:56,395 --> 00:01:00,115
From the busiest street in the city, you
can be on a quiet mountain trail in less

21
00:01:00,155 --> 00:01:00,945
than an hour.

22
00:01:01,831 --> 00:01:05,971
So almost all of those seven and a half
million people live on a narrow strip of

23
00:01:06,011 --> 00:01:08,581
flat land along the coast
and around the harbor.

24
00:01:08,851 --> 00:01:11,641
When you can't build out,
there's only one direction left.

25
00:01:12,561 --> 00:01:14,171
It wasn't always this crowded.

26
00:01:14,661 --> 00:01:17,951
After the Second World War, and
especially after nineteen forty-nine,

27
00:01:18,301 --> 00:01:21,871
hundreds of thousands of refugees
poured in from mainland China.

28
00:01:22,141 --> 00:01:25,831
With nowhere else to go, many of them
built wooden shacks on the hillsides.

29
00:01:28,230 --> 00:01:29,220
So the city went up.

30
00:01:29,525 --> 00:01:33,095
Hong Kong has more skyscrapers
than any other city on Earth.

31
00:01:33,345 --> 00:01:37,455
In early twenty twenty-five, it had six
hundred and sixty-nine buildings at least a

32
00:01:37,495 --> 00:01:39,115
hundred and fifty meters tall.

33
00:01:39,345 --> 00:01:40,275
More than New York.

34
00:01:40,445 --> 00:01:41,195
More than Dubai.

35
00:01:42,286 --> 00:01:43,776
And most of them aren't offices.

36
00:01:44,146 --> 00:01:44,836
They're homes.

37
00:01:45,366 --> 00:01:47,656
Rows of residential towers, forty, fifty,

38
00:01:47,696 --> 00:01:51,986
sixty floors high, standing so close
together that neighbors can see into each

39
00:01:52,026 --> 00:01:52,816
other's kitchens.

40
00:01:53,875 --> 00:01:55,765
The most famous is in Quarry Bay.

41
00:01:56,115 --> 00:02:00,775
Locals call it the Monster Building: five
old blocks joined into one giant wall of

42
00:02:00,815 --> 00:02:03,405
tiny apartments, air
conditioners and laundry.

43
00:02:03,775 --> 00:02:05,835
It was finished in the
early nineteen seventies,

44
00:02:05,875 --> 00:02:07,905
and thousands of people still live there.

45
00:02:08,713 --> 00:02:12,423
For decades, many of these towers
were built and repaired using bamboo.

46
00:02:12,973 --> 00:02:15,993
Workers tie bamboo poles
together with plastic strips,

47
00:02:16,033 --> 00:02:18,543
climbing dozens of floors
with no metal at all.

48
00:02:18,753 --> 00:02:22,793
It's light, cheap and fast, and for a long
time it covered most building sites in

49
00:02:22,833 --> 00:02:23,323
the city.

50
00:02:24,182 --> 00:02:28,702
But in November twenty twenty-five, a fire
at Wang Fuk Court in Tai Po spread across

51
00:02:28,742 --> 00:02:32,592
seven towers wrapped in scaffolding
and green netting during renovation.

52
00:02:33,182 --> 00:02:35,112
A hundred and sixty-eight people died.

53
00:02:35,342 --> 00:02:38,822
An inquiry later found that flammable
netting and foam boards helped the fire

54
00:02:38,862 --> 00:02:39,352
spread.

55
00:02:39,708 --> 00:02:43,688
Even before that tragedy, the government
had started moving public building projects

56
00:02:43,728 --> 00:02:45,938
from bamboo to metal scaffolding.

57
00:02:46,368 --> 00:02:49,518
One of the city's most famous
sights is slowly disappearing.

58
00:02:50,475 --> 00:02:53,605
And every one of those towers
has to survive typhoons.

59
00:02:53,775 --> 00:02:56,465
When the Observatory
raises signal number eight,

60
00:02:56,555 --> 00:03:01,085
offices and schools close, and the whole
city waits indoors until the storm passes.

61
00:03:03,546 --> 00:03:05,476
But building up isn't the only trick.

62
00:03:05,606 --> 00:03:08,796
When Hong Kong ran out of flat
ground, it simply made more.

63
00:03:09,470 --> 00:03:13,550
For more than a century, the city has been
filling in the sea with rock and sand to

64
00:03:13,590 --> 00:03:14,440
make new land.

65
00:03:14,710 --> 00:03:15,720
It's called reclamation.

66
00:03:16,774 --> 00:03:21,084
Today, only about seven percent of
Hong Kong's land was made this way.

67
00:03:21,234 --> 00:03:25,104
But that seven percent holds some of
the most valuable places in the city,

68
00:03:25,174 --> 00:03:27,684
including a huge share of
its business districts.

69
00:03:28,114 --> 00:03:29,644
The famous waterfront skyline?

70
00:03:29,914 --> 00:03:31,924
Much of it stands where
ships used to sail.

71
00:03:32,726 --> 00:03:36,696
So much of Victoria Harbour has been
filled in that in nineteen ninety-seven,

72
00:03:37,146 --> 00:03:39,756
the city passed a law to
protect what's left of it.

73
00:03:40,046 --> 00:03:44,256
Today, the harbour can only be reclaimed
if there's an overriding public need.

74
00:03:45,155 --> 00:03:47,265
The biggest project of
all was the airport.

75
00:03:47,535 --> 00:03:50,825
The old airport, Kai Tak, sat
right in the middle of the city.

76
00:03:51,475 --> 00:03:55,315
Planes came in so low over the apartment
blocks that passengers could look into

77
00:03:55,355 --> 00:03:56,565
people's living rooms.

78
00:03:57,494 --> 00:04:01,364
Pilots flew straight at a hill painted
with a red and white checkerboard,

79
00:04:01,414 --> 00:04:04,724
then made a sharp turn at the last
moment to line up with the runway.

80
00:04:05,234 --> 00:04:08,764
It was one of the most famous and most
nerve-racking landings in the world.

81
00:04:09,605 --> 00:04:11,955
In nineteen ninety-eight, Kai Tak closed.

82
00:04:12,385 --> 00:04:16,385
The new airport was built on an island that
was flattened and extended into the sea,

83
00:04:16,425 --> 00:04:19,675
with more than nine square
kilometers of brand-new land.

84
00:04:20,575 --> 00:04:22,185
And the city still wants more.

85
00:04:22,375 --> 00:04:26,595
One plan, off Lantau Island, would
create around a thousand hectares of new

86
00:04:26,635 --> 00:04:27,885
artificial islands.

87
00:04:28,055 --> 00:04:31,245
But it's so expensive that, for
now, it has been put on hold.

88
00:04:33,749 --> 00:04:36,179
So the city grows up, and
it grows into the sea.

89
00:04:36,329 --> 00:04:37,939
And still, there isn't enough room.

90
00:04:38,494 --> 00:04:42,494
For more than fifteen years in a row, Hong
Kong has been ranked the least affordable

91
00:04:42,534 --> 00:04:44,224
housing market in the world.

92
00:04:44,534 --> 00:04:48,414
A typical home costs around fourteen
years of a typical household's income,

93
00:04:48,454 --> 00:04:51,224
without spending a single
dollar on anything else.

94
00:04:52,093 --> 00:04:55,123
That's why so many people
live in subdivided flats.

95
00:04:55,453 --> 00:04:58,693
An ordinary apartment is
split into several tiny units,

96
00:04:58,733 --> 00:05:00,083
each with its own family.

97
00:05:00,433 --> 00:05:02,963
Sometimes there's a bed,
a kitchen and a toilet,

98
00:05:03,033 --> 00:05:05,383
all in a space the size of a parking spot.

99
00:05:06,307 --> 00:05:10,587
In the last census, more than two hundred
thousand people lived in these subdivided

100
00:05:10,627 --> 00:05:11,117
flats.

101
00:05:11,427 --> 00:05:14,117
A typical unit was only
about ten square meters.

102
00:05:15,048 --> 00:05:17,558
Even outside those flats, space is tight.

103
00:05:18,048 --> 00:05:21,908
Across the whole city, the typical person
has about sixteen square meters of living

104
00:05:21,948 --> 00:05:22,578
space.

105
00:05:23,362 --> 00:05:27,172
At the extreme end are so-called
cage homes and coffin homes:

106
00:05:27,422 --> 00:05:30,022
bunk spaces barely big
enough to lie down in,

107
00:05:30,062 --> 00:05:31,032
rented by the month.

108
00:05:31,733 --> 00:05:34,583
Public housing in Hong
Kong began with a disaster.

109
00:05:35,013 --> 00:05:36,583
On Christmas night, nineteen fifty-three,

110
00:05:36,913 --> 00:05:40,853
a fire swept through the hillside shacks
at Shek Kip Mei and left more than fifty

111
00:05:40,893 --> 00:05:42,163
thousand people homeless.

112
00:05:42,513 --> 00:05:46,003
To rehouse them, the government
built rows of simple concrete blocks.

113
00:05:46,193 --> 00:05:48,623
They became the city's
first public housing estate.

114
00:05:48,949 --> 00:05:51,219
The government's answer is public housing.

115
00:05:51,549 --> 00:05:56,449
Today, about forty-five percent of Hong
Kong's population lives in public rental or

116
00:05:56,489 --> 00:06:00,199
subsidized flats, in those huge
estates of identical towers.

117
00:06:01,179 --> 00:06:03,749
But the wait for a public
rental flat is long.

118
00:06:03,979 --> 00:06:07,889
In recent years, the average family
has waited around five years for one.

119
00:06:10,103 --> 00:06:12,803
Now, when this many people
live this close together,

120
00:06:12,843 --> 00:06:15,253
moving them around becomes
a problem of its own.

121
00:06:15,523 --> 00:06:18,373
And this is where Hong
Kong is surprisingly good.

122
00:06:19,215 --> 00:06:22,805
The metro, the MTR, carries
millions of journeys every day.

123
00:06:23,015 --> 00:06:27,115
And year after year, it reports that
about ninety-nine point nine percent of

124
00:06:27,155 --> 00:06:28,965
passenger journeys arrive on time.

125
00:06:29,890 --> 00:06:31,440
Paying is just as fast.

126
00:06:31,850 --> 00:06:35,990
Since nineteen ninety-seven, people have
tapped a contactless card called Octopus on

127
00:06:36,030 --> 00:06:38,960
trains, buses, ferries, and even in shops.

128
00:06:39,510 --> 00:06:42,220
It was one of the first systems
like it anywhere in the world.

129
00:06:43,098 --> 00:06:45,408
Then there are the old
ways that never left.

130
00:06:45,778 --> 00:06:49,748
Double-decker trams have rattled along
Hong Kong Island since nineteen oh four.

131
00:06:50,258 --> 00:06:54,088
The Star Ferry has been crossing Victoria
Harbour since the eighteen eighties.

132
00:06:54,838 --> 00:06:56,868
Because the city climbs
straight up a mountain,

133
00:06:56,938 --> 00:06:59,048
it even has escalators instead of streets.

134
00:06:59,558 --> 00:07:03,218
The Central to Mid-Levels escalator runs
about eight hundred meters up the hillside

135
00:07:03,258 --> 00:07:05,628
and climbs about a hundred
and thirty-five meters.

136
00:07:05,978 --> 00:07:09,518
It opened in nineteen ninety-three, and
it's one of the longest outdoor covered

137
00:07:09,558 --> 00:07:11,108
escalator systems in the world.

138
00:07:11,487 --> 00:07:14,297
In the morning, it runs
downhill, toward the offices.

139
00:07:14,727 --> 00:07:18,577
Later in the day, it switches direction,
and carries people back up the mountain.

140
00:07:20,975 --> 00:07:22,725
So how crowded can a place get?

141
00:07:22,895 --> 00:07:25,125
Hong Kong has held that
record more than once.

142
00:07:25,663 --> 00:07:30,083
The Mong Kok district in Kowloon was once
named by Guinness World Records as the

143
00:07:30,123 --> 00:07:34,683
most densely populated place on Earth:
about a hundred and thirty thousand people

144
00:07:34,723 --> 00:07:36,033
per square kilometer.

145
00:07:36,266 --> 00:07:40,236
But even that was nothing compared to
what used to stand a few kilometers away:

146
00:07:40,506 --> 00:07:41,836
the Kowloon Walled City.

147
00:07:42,570 --> 00:07:46,550
It was a single block of buildings, packed
onto an area smaller than four football

148
00:07:46,590 --> 00:07:47,320
fields.

149
00:07:47,710 --> 00:07:51,670
In nineteen eighty-seven, a government
survey counted about thirty-three thousand

150
00:07:51,710 --> 00:07:52,940
people living inside.

151
00:07:53,210 --> 00:07:55,760
That's more than a million
people per square kilometer.

152
00:07:56,073 --> 00:07:59,393
The buildings grew into each other
until they became one solid mass,

153
00:07:59,433 --> 00:08:02,233
with narrow, dark passages
running through it,

154
00:08:02,273 --> 00:08:05,043
lit by electric lamps, even
in the middle of the day.

155
00:08:05,915 --> 00:08:08,805
Inside, there were homes,
factories, dentists,

156
00:08:08,895 --> 00:08:10,545
noodle makers and food stalls.

157
00:08:10,835 --> 00:08:13,315
And for decades, it was
almost a place without rules,

158
00:08:13,355 --> 00:08:16,145
because neither Britain nor
China fully governed it.

159
00:08:16,958 --> 00:08:18,988
Demolition began in nineteen ninety-three.

160
00:08:19,598 --> 00:08:21,748
Today, there's a quiet
park where it once stood.

161
00:08:23,826 --> 00:08:27,016
So, how do seven and a half
million people fit into Hong Kong?

162
00:08:27,618 --> 00:08:30,608
They build up, higher than
almost anywhere else on Earth.

163
00:08:30,858 --> 00:08:32,408
They make new land out of the sea.

164
00:08:32,698 --> 00:08:34,918
They live in some of the
smallest homes in the world,

165
00:08:34,958 --> 00:08:38,858
and they move through the city on one of
the most precise transport systems ever

166
00:08:38,898 --> 00:08:39,348
built.

167
00:08:40,198 --> 00:08:42,648
And all of that so that,
just outside the towers,

168
00:08:42,998 --> 00:08:44,868
almost half of the land can stay wild.

169
00:08:45,504 --> 00:08:48,314
Hong Kong is a city with
mountains on one side,

170
00:08:48,364 --> 00:08:50,654
the sea on the other,
and nowhere to go but up.

171
00:08:51,194 --> 00:08:53,934
So, could you live in a home
the size of a parking space,

172
00:08:53,974 --> 00:08:55,704
if this was the view outside?

173
00:08:55,954 --> 00:08:59,384
Tell me in the comments, and tell me
which city we should explain next.

174
00:08:59,634 --> 00:09:00,804
This was How It's Run.
